            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Why Your Multi-Account Campaigns Are Always in Chaos</h2>
<p>When marketing teams managing overseas campaigns handle more than 5 advertising accounts, common scenarios include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Campaign managers don't know which account corresponds to which product line</li>
<li>A channel account suddenly gets suspended, causing team-wide emergency response</li>
<li>At month-end reconciliation, budget spending and order data don't match</li>
<li>New team members taking over accounts have no idea where to start</li>
</ul>
<p>These problems aren't about "insufficient tools" but <strong>lack of layered account management mechanisms</strong>. The following checklist helps teams establish a clear account management framework.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Define Account Classification Standards</h2>
<p>Before allocating permissions and budgets, answer this question: <strong>What dimension do you use to classify accounts?</strong></p>
<p>Recommended layered logic based on business attributes:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Layer Dimension</th>
<th>Applicable Scenarios</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>By Product Line</td>
<td>E-commerce teams with multiple SKUs or categories</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>By Platform</td>
<td>Separate management for Google/Meta/TikTok</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>By Region</td>
<td>Overseas teams covering multiple markets</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>By Objective</td>
<td>Brand awareness vs. direct conversion separated</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>By Account Type</td>
<td>Main spending accounts vs. testing accounts vs. backup accounts</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Checklist</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] List all current advertising accounts (including suspended ones)</li>
<li>[ ] Add classification tags to each account</li>
<li>[ ] Confirm layered logic is approved by team leadership</li>
<li>[ ] Establish naming conventions: new account names must include "Platform-Product-Region" info</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Allocate Account Permissions and Roles</h2>
<p>Permission chaos leads to operational errors and unclear responsibilities. Recommended role settings:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Admin</strong>: Highest permissions, responsible for account opening, topping up, and risk control</li>
<li><strong>Campaign Manager</strong>: Operates ad campaigns, no financial access</li>
<li><strong>Designer</strong>: Views creative performance only, no account settings access</li>
<li><strong>Finance</strong>: Views billing and spending data only</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Checklist</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] List all current admin accounts for each account</li>
<li>[ ] Clean up permissions for departed employees</li>
<li>[ ] Set standard permission templates for common platforms (Google/Meta/TikTok)</li>
<li>[ ] Confirm all accounts have 2FA enabled</li>
<li>[ ] Maintain permission change records; log time and reason for each adjustment</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Establish Budget Allocation Rules</h2>
<p>The core contradiction in multi-account campaigns: <strong>How to distribute limited budgets among accounts?</strong></p>
<p>Recommended "Base Quota + Performance Floating" mechanism:</p>
<ol>
<li>Set monthly base budget for each account (ensure testing stability)</li>
<li>Set performance trigger conditions: if ROAS &gt; 3, can apply for 20% budget increase</li>
<li>Set account budget caps: prevent single account overspending</li>
<li>Reserve 10%-15% as flexible budget for sudden campaign needs</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Checklist</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Set daily and monthly budget caps for each account</li>
<li>[ ] Clarify budget adjustment approval process (who applies, who approves)</li>
<li>[ ] Establish daily budget consumption monitoring; set balance warning threshold at 20%</li>
<li>[ ] Weekly review account spending progress, compare plan vs. actual</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Build Account Health Monitoring Mechanism</h2>
<p>Account suspension is one of the biggest risks in overseas advertising. Daily checks recommended:</p>
<p><strong>Account Health Checklist</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Check if login IP is abnormal (judge if restricted)</li>
<li>[ ] Verify account balance is sufficient</li>
<li>[ ] Check ad review status for any disapprovals</li>
<li>[ ] Verify payment method is valid</li>
<li>[ ] Check for new violation records</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Risk Account Isolation Strategy</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>If an account shows high-risk alerts, immediately reduce budget to 50%</li>
<li>If rejected twice consecutively, suspend account and start appeal</li>
<li>Suspended account spending data must be retained for 90 days for financial reconciliation</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 5: Build Account Documentation and Handover System</h2>
<p>Multi-account management's biggest fear is "chaos after personnel changes." Each account needs a basic document:</p>
<p><strong>Required Account Profile Contents</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>Account ID and login link</li>
<li>Account opening date and campaign history</li>
<li>Current campaign strategy and core KPIs</li>
<li>Common targeting countries and bidding strategies</li>
<li>Payment methods and top-up records</li>
<li>Historical issues (suspension reasons, appeal results, etc.)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Checklist</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] All account profiles stored in shared document library (Notion/Feishu)</li>
<li>[ ] Each handover requires written records with recipient signature</li>
<li>[ ] Update account profiles quarterly</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Quick Execution Checklist: 5 Things to Do Today</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Export account lists</strong>: Export account lists from Google Ads, Meta Business Manager, and TikTok Ads Manager</li>
<li><strong>Check permission status</strong>: Clean up accounts inactive for more than 90 days</li>
<li><strong>Set balance warnings</strong>: Ensure all accounts have notifications enabled</li>
<li><strong>Build shared documents</strong>: Create account profile templates and assign to each campaign manager</li>
<li><strong>Align with finance</strong>: Confirm consistent data sources for spending across accounts to avoid reconciliation discrepancies</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<p>Layered account management is not a one-time project but requires continuous iteration with business changes. Quarterly account architecture reviews are recommended to check if layered logic remains applicable.</p>
<p>If you encounter specific issues in account management (such as multi-account top-up confusion or balance warning system setup), feel free to discuss specific scenarios.</p>
